WEBVTT

title-1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.620
Loopback Transformer:

title-2
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:05.970
towards a unified view of
residual looped Transformers.

live-3
00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:07.940
First,

live-4
00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:08.760
a live read:

live-5
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:13.460
a standard Transformer and Loopback
read the same 4,096 held-out tokens.

live-6
00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.940
Loopback stores 1.0B parameters,

live-7
00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.140
the standard model 1.4B,

live-8
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.600
and Loopback's running loss ends lower.

hook-9
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:30.350
Looped Transformers save parameters by
running the same blocks again and again.

hook-10
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.050
But sharing alone doesn't decide how
all that repeated computation is reused.

analysis-11
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.300
Think of residual
connections as depth memory.

analysis-12
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.920
Addition lets the total update
weight keep growing with depth,

analysis-13
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.700
and a joint softmax makes the
input compete with the updates.

method-14
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.930
Loopback decouples the two:

method-15
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.290
it stores each block's update,

method-16
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.950
keeps the input on its own path,

method-17
00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.030
and lets decay set the total weight
while attention allocates it.

scale-18
00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:03.020
The result:

scale-19
00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.740
from 191M to 3B parameters,

scale-20
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.439
Loopback reaches lower loss and a higher

scale-21
00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:12.460
thirteen-task average than
the standard Transformer,

scale-22
00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.940
with only 40–75% of its parameters.

nine-23
00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:22.760
Against the standard Transformer and
seven other looped residual designs,

nine-24
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.706
Loopback has the lowest
loss and the highest

nine-25
00:01:25.706 --> 00:01:28.220
thirteen-task average at all three scales.

math-26
00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.760
At 1.4B and 3B,

math-27
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.180
it also predicts math and
code solutions better,

math-28
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:36.840
and at 3B,

math-29
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.320
word-problem accuracy
rises from 2.6% to 6.4%.

visits-30
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.780
Run more loops at
inference than in training,

visits-31
00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:51.980
and Loopback degrades far less than
Plain Loop at all three scales.

ablation-32
00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:54.950
Both controls matter:

ablation-33
00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:55.980
without attention,

ablation-34
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:56.980
the loss rises;

ablation-35
00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:58.260
without decay,

ablation-36
00:01:58.390 --> 00:01:59.620
it rises much more.

ablation-37
00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.680
And the gains hold across initializations.

depth-38
00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.000
At a fixed parameter budget,

depth-39
00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.340
deeper and narrower standard
Transformers lose ground,

depth-40
00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.860
while Loopback's margin grows with depth.

eff-41
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.170
The cost is small:

eff-42
00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.690
reading the stored updates
adds under 0.1% in FLOPs,

eff-43
00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.210
and at 3B,

eff-44
00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.990
training runs within 6% of the
standard Transformer's speed.

outro-45
00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.110
Loopback stores updates,

outro-46
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.470
keeps the input on its own path,

outro-47
00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.470
and lets decay and attention
control their weight:

outro-48
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.690
lower loss with fewer parameters.
